  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nThe current synoptic setup over the forecast area remains benign, \nwith prominent ridging over much of the central CONUS framed by a \npositively tilted trough axis across the eastern CONUS and a mostly \nneutral tilt trough axis across the western CONUS. Surface high \npressure centered over the eastern Great Lakes is helping contribute \nto light southerly winds, while a dense layer of stratus off to the \nwest follows a plume of low-level moisture surging northward across \nthe central Plains. During the upcoming work week, the ridge axis \nwill begin to shift eastward as the western CONUS trough lifts \nacross the Intermountain West. Mid-level ridging will amplify \nagainst the positively tilted trough situated over the Gulf Coast, \nkeeping surface high pressure situated over the broader eastern \nGreat Lakes and Appalachia. As the trough lifts across the northern \nGreat Plains, a developing surface cyclone off the lee of the \nRockies will enhance the surface pressure gradient across the \ncentral CONUS. This tightening pressure gradient will contribute to \na breezy short term forecast, with southerly winds today across \neastern KS/western MO around 10-15 mph and gusts up to 20 mph. As \nthe surface cyclone continues to deepen and lift across the northern \nPlains with its parent trough, the pressure gradient will continue \nto tighten with surface high pressure remaining in place over the \nAppalachians. This will continue to enhance surface winds, \nincreasing to 20-25 mph by Wednesday. An enhanced low-level jet \nstreak accompanying the trough will also increase wind gusts earlier \nin the day, with gusts up to 30-35 mph possible, especially across \nfar northwest MO. With greater forcing for ascent remaining to the \nnorth of the forecast area, precipitation chances with this passing \nsystem remain negligible.\n\nAs the trough continues lifting across the northern Great Lakes, mid-\nlevel ridging will build back in across the central CONUS. Ongoing \nWAA in the low levels of the atmosphere with continued \nsouth/southwesterly winds will contribute to temperatures rising \ninto the mid/upper 60s and low 70s for the latter half of the work \nweek. Precipitation chances return by the weekend, with the \nprogression of a dynamic trough from the western CONUS into the \ncentral Plains by Saturday. The primary forcing mechanism for \nprecipitation chances (30-60%) looks to be a cold front draping down \nfrom the northwest. Although shear looks to be sufficient for \nthe development of organized thunderstorm activity, available \nconvective energy looks incredibly marginal at this time. After\nthe passage of the cold front, temperatures will fall back to \nthe upper 40s/lower 50s as surface high pressure builds in from \nthe north with mid- level ridging. The remainder of the long \nterm forecast remains dry, with temperatures continuing to fall \ninto the low/mid 40s by early next week.",
